{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"6ec154faf7a79c74","type":"group","x":-720,"y":520,"width":260,"height":180,"label":"L2"},
		{"id":"bf26e9a887e0f7cf","type":"group","x":-719,"y":1420,"width":259,"height":175,"label":"S6"},
		{"id":"89c835a738c57b2a","type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Axial Coding Map\n\nCore axial relation:\n\nConditions: task novelty, prior exposure, modelling-family complexity, rationale visibility, timing of examples, feedback access, and assessment pressure.\n\nActions / interactions: lecturers demonstrate, decompose, compare, fade support, and withhold complete answers; students decode, imitate, adapt, compare, and seek confirmation.\n\nOutcomes: examples can build judgement, confidence, transfer, and rationale quality, but can also produce copying, example dependence, weak transfer, or false certainty.","x":900,"y":-105,"width":400,"height":350},
		{"id":"f354c38714f5d468","type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Triangulation / Negative Cases\n\nTriangulation task:\n\nCompare lecturer intentions with student uses of examples. Treat worked examples as helpful only when evidence shows reasoning visibility, comparison, or transfer beyond surface copying.\n\nMain caution: the same example can function as scaffolding or as a template, depending on timing, explanation, variation, and whether students later justify their own decisions.\n\nOpen checks remain around timing, partial vs complete examples, same-domain transfer, and modelling-family variation.","x":1500,"y":-112,"width":380,"height":360},
		{"id":"ac1aa3ecefd8d78d","type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Topic Overview\n\nAnalytic focus: worked examples as scaffolding for representational judgement.\n\nThe canvas asks when examples help students move from problem text to defensible models, and when examples instead encourage copying or dependence.\n\nKey distinction: examples are strongest when they expose modelling rationale, alternatives, rejected choices, granularity decisions, and quality checks. They are weaker when they only show a polished final diagram.","x":-600,"y":-114,"width":331,"height":340},
		{"id":"5faa76346b41e3e8","type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Open Coding Evidence\n\nUse the attached comparison note and memos to code incidents into:\n\n- Challenges: starting, relation choice, granularity, transfer, over-modelling, uncertainty.\n- Strategies: example decoding, analogy, comparison, decomposition, fading, feedback-seeking.\n- Conditions: timing, rationale visibility, task similarity, prior exposure, complexity.\n- Supports: complete examples, contrastive examples, partial examples, teacher demonstration, semantic checks.\n- Consequences: judgement, confidence, transfer, copying, dependence, revision quality.","x":106,"y":-104,"width":360,"height":360},
		{"id":"5132e26a61cb8b47","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_L2M3.md","x":-694,"y":637,"width":217,"height":50},
		{"id":"af6238a68cf92f93","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_L1M2.md","x":-696,"y":737,"width":211,"height":50},
		{"id":"f15cd7e112302a6a","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_L3M4.md","x":-693,"y":820,"width":214,"height":50},
		{"id":"1b3dc07855a679c3","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_L4M6.md","x":-696,"y":900,"width":214,"height":50},
		{"id":"b2de2197522725a1","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_L2M2.md","x":-694,"y":560,"width":217,"height":50},
		{"id":"d8c16e90b0274b97","type":"text","text":"# Lecturers\n\nLecturer evidence frames examples as staged support for autonomy: demonstration, decomposition, comparison, counterexamples, and rationale-based feedback.\n\nTension: lecturers use structure, but still want students to make situated modelling decisions rather than follow a fixed recipe.","x":-377,"y":420,"width":300,"height":210},
		{"id":"3db6458b6c2a51fd","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_S6M5.md","x":-699,"y":1525,"width":220,"height":50},
		{"id":"68777d5ae8ade8ed","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_S6M3.md","x":-699,"y":1440,"width":220,"height":50},
		{"id":"ee2874c113eaddc2","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_S1M5.md","x":-722,"y":1015,"width":211,"height":50},
		{"id":"87efffde5296e1b2","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_S2M3.md","x":-722,"y":1092,"width":211,"height":50},
		{"id":"c7bd98aa06fd3195","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_S3M5.md","x":-720,"y":1167,"width":209,"height":50},
		{"id":"3e68f693457dd8e5","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_S4M3.md","x":-716,"y":1250,"width":205,"height":50},
		{"id":"4e0cf334b940432d","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_S5M3.md","x":-716,"y":1330,"width":205,"height":50},
		{"id":"163a30b28365c764","type":"text","text":"# Students\n\nStudent evidence asks for examples that make reasoning visible: text-to-model translation, relation choice, granularity calibration, quality-to-design traceability, and complete examples before difficult practice.\n\nTension: examples help students start and calibrate, but may also become copied patterns.","x":-294,"y":1015,"width":330,"height":220},
		{"id":"9cdb9071c654fa5d","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/04_cross_case_analysis/comparison_worked_examples.md","x":240,"y":307,"width":364,"height":480},
		{"id":"84b1117dd6d0d042","type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Source Comparison\n\nAlignment:\n- Lecturers and students both treat examples as a route into tacit modelling process.\n- Both sides connect examples to starting, relation choice, granularity, and confidence.\n\nDifference:\n- Lecturers emphasise staged autonomy and avoiding fixed recipes.\n- Students emphasise complete, explicit, and transferable examples because partial materials leave correctness uncertain.\n\nAnalytic use: compare whether examples reveal judgement or simply provide a visible pattern to reproduce.","x":247,"y":977,"width":350,"height":360},
		{"id":"13fb0e273e0c555c","x":-211,"y":-686,"width":330,"height":150,"type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Strategies\n\nLecturer strategies: demonstrate step by step, use counterexamples, decompose hard tasks, intervene when many are stuck, compare alternatives, fade support.\n\nStudent strategies: decode completed examples, use analogy, compare too-detailed and too-abstract versions, seek feedback to confirm interpretation."},
		{"id":"9a1e470e8eed992b","x":-211,"y":-615,"width":330,"height":145,"type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Conditions\n\nExamples are more useful when they show rationale, vary surface details, connect theory and practice, and arrive before students are fully blocked.\n\nThey are riskier when tasks are too similar, examples are polished final answers, or assessment rewards reproduction over justification."},
		{"id":"5f7f657d50f61d04","x":-211,"y":-760,"width":330,"height":145,"type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Challenges\n\nStudents struggle to start independently, choose relationships, calibrate granularity, translate text into model elements, connect qualities to design decisions, and transfer across modelling families.\n\nExamples can also create copying, dependence, or false certainty."},
		{"id":"bdcd394cbf219976","x":-211,"y":-445,"width":330,"height":145,"type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Consequences\n\nPositive: better first moves, relation choice, abstraction judgement, confidence, traceability, and revision.\n\nNegative: surface imitation, waiting for similar examples, weak transfer, suppressed exploration, and treating one diagram as the only valid answer."},
		{"id":"6093902f9106bb5a","x":-211,"y":-525,"width":330,"height":145,"type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Supports\n\nUseful supports include complete worked examples with commentary, contrastive examples, partial examples, evolving cases, rationale notes, quality checks, teacher feedback, and lightweight trace links across model families."},
		{"id":"04d053e4191b9c5c","x":680,"y":-700,"width":330,"height":150,"type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Axial Conditions\n\nPrior exposure, confidence, task novelty, diagram type, relation ambiguity, granularity pressure, tool fragmentation, feedback availability, and whether rationale is visible shape how students use examples."},
		{"id":"6ae02f9b4a32c47e","x":680,"y":-400,"width":330,"height":150,"type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Outcomes\n\nWorked examples reduce representational uncertainty when they make decision criteria observable and reusable.\n\nOutcomes include independence, transferable judgement, better rationale, and revision, or dependence, copying, and weak transfer."},
		{"id":"ad11950ce84e3122","x":680,"y":-560,"width":330,"height":150,"type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Actions / Interactions\n\nStudents compare, imitate, adapt, decode relation meaning, calibrate detail, and seek confirmation.\n\nLecturers model reasoning, use similar tasks, show counterexamples, decompose problems, withhold solutions, and provide rationale-focused feedback."},
		{"id":"d0e97710d8a8b626","type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Alignments\n\nStrong alignment: examples matter because modelling decisions are partly tacit.\n\nL1/L2/L3 show examples and process support. S1/S2/S3/S4/S5/S6 confirm examples help with text-to-model moves, relationships, granularity, and traceability.","x":1275,"y":480,"width":330,"height":150},
		{"id":"d082853b55d520a4","type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Divergences\n\nLecturers worry about preserving autonomy and situated judgement. Students often want fuller examples earlier.\n\nL4 avoids giving direct solutions in advance, while S3 asks for complete examples before advanced labs. This is a timing and dependence tension, not a simple disagreement.","x":1565,"y":480,"width":330,"height":160},
		{"id":"4962253e52cfbaa5","type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Open Evidence Checks\n\nCheck:\n- same-domain vs cross-domain transfer;\n- examples before first attempt vs after struggle;\n- partial examples vs complete solutions;\n- variation by diagram type;\n- whether stated preference for examples leads to later independent performance.","x":1870,"y":480,"width":330,"height":160},
		{"id":"764ceb8c22a30c10","x":2230,"y":450,"width":330,"height":145,"type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Teaching Implications\n\nDesign examples to show reasoning, not only answers. Include alternatives, rejected options, granularity comparisons, quality checks, and follow-up tasks with changed surface features.\n\nFade support toward student justification."},
		{"id":"11010974cf5ba23c","x":2230,"y":248,"width":330,"height":145,"type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Emerging Pattern\n\nWorked examples support modelling judgement when they expose the decisions behind a representation and are followed by varied practice.\n\nThey are least productive when they become answer templates detached from rationale."},
		{"id":"abfb0078e11c3bda","x":2230,"y":350,"width":330,"height":145,"type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Boundaries / Cautions\n\nDo not treat example preference as proof of learning effect. The evidence shows perceived support and plausible transfer mechanisms, but later performance would be needed to validate independence.\n\nCopying risk remains a boundary condition."},
		{"id":"33c2071eb897dd7d","type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Refined Interpretation\n\nProvisional interpretation:\n\nWorked examples reduce representational uncertainty by making tacit modelling choices observable. They foster independence when learners examine alternatives, apply explicit criteria, practise on varied tasks, and gradually assume responsibility for decisions.\n\nThey reinforce dependence when students can reproduce a visible pattern without understanding or testing its rationale.","x":2780,"y":-112,"width":360,"height":320},
		{"id":"19ed6f96148e69e3","type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Links to Broader Theory\n\nBroader theory role:\n\nWorked examples connect pedagogical scaffolding to representational judgement. They show how learners move between uncertainty, imitation, adaptation, justification, and independent modelling.\n\nKeep these links provisional until more topic canvases are built and compared.","x":3640,"y":-118,"width":342,"height":300},
		{"id":"1255846aafedd137","x":3440,"y":300,"width":330,"height":120,"type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Core Phenomenon Link\n\nWorked examples make representational uncertainty manageable by externalising modelling judgement: what to include, omit, connect, refine, and justify."},
		{"id":"9bac72b3f421e475","x":3440,"y":420,"width":330,"height":130,"type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Category Links\n\nCandidate category links: representational challenges, learner strategies, pedagogical scaffolding, feedback, assessment architecture, modelling outcomes, and social/contextual conditions."},
		{"id":"2903ae31055d15c9","x":3440,"y":540,"width":330,"height":130,"type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Response Mode Links\n\nExamples can move students from avoidance or uncertainty into imitation, then adaptation, comparison, justification, and revision. The risk is stopping at imitation."},
		{"id":"1f82b9c8c348021c","x":3440,"y":640,"width":330,"height":130,"type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Negative Boundary Tests\n\nBoundary tests: copying without rationale, polished solutions shown too early, weak transfer to different domains, over-reliance on similar examples, and examples that suppress exploration."},
		{"id":"79476a615b2b19ca","x":3440,"y":760,"width":330,"height":145,"type":"text","text":"Worked examples - Theoretical Sampling Questions\n\nAsk later: Do partial examples produce more independence than full examples? When should examples appear? Which diagram types need contrastive examples most? Does example use improve later unsupported modelling?"}
	],
